1
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:06,796
Welcome to Kubernetes From Zero.

2
00:00:06,796 --> 00:00:10,922
In this course you will install Kubernetes four different ways.

3
00:00:10,922 --> 00:00:14,524
First by hand, with kube admin, on two Ubuntu machines.

4
00:00:14,524 --> 00:00:18,520
That is the one that teaches you how Kubernetes really works.

5
00:00:19,067 --> 00:00:23,916
Then with Minikube, a complete cluster inside one container on your laptop.

6
00:00:24,467 --> 00:00:29,211
Then with MicroK8s, a whole Kubernetes in one package with one command.

7
00:00:29,767 --> 00:00:36,948
And finally on Amazon EKS, where AWS runs the control plane for you, and every hour costs real money.

8
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:39,548
Every time, the goal is the same:

9
00:00:39,548 --> 00:00:43,333
a cluster you have verified, a test application that answers,

10
00:00:43,333 --> 00:00:47,739
a failure you found and fixed yourself, and a clean machine at the end.

11
00:00:47,739 --> 00:00:51,276
By the end you will know which method to choose, and why.

12
00:00:53,033 --> 00:00:54,732
This is not a slide show.

13
00:00:54,732 --> 00:00:57,177
We will work the way engineers work.

14
00:00:57,177 --> 00:01:00,370
First understand what you are about to install.

15
00:01:00,933 --> 00:01:05,913
Then install it, and verify it, with the same checklist on every cluster.

16
00:01:06,467 --> 00:01:08,142
Then break it on purpose.

17
00:01:08,142 --> 00:01:13,571
Eight troubleshooting labs, each with a real symptom, an investigation and a fix.

18
00:01:14,133 --> 00:01:19,293
And finally compare the four methods and clean everything up, including the cloud resources.

19
00:01:21,033 --> 00:01:24,897
Everything in this video comes from one free repository on GitHub.

20
00:01:24,897 --> 00:01:30,693
The docs explain the concepts, and each installation method has its own lesson with a cleanup page.

21
00:01:31,233 --> 00:01:34,241
The troubleshooting folder has the eight failure labs,

22
00:01:34,241 --> 00:01:37,583
and labs and capstone have challenges with hidden solutions.

23
00:01:38,133 --> 00:01:39,455
And this is important:

24
00:01:39,455 --> 00:01:44,323
every command in the lessons is executed automatically on fresh virtual machines.

25
00:01:44,323 --> 00:01:48,109
Every terminal in this video shows real output from those runs.

26
00:01:48,109 --> 00:01:50,513
Nothing is typed by hand for the camera.

27
00:01:52,267 --> 00:01:54,215
Running one container is easy.

28
00:01:54,215 --> 00:01:58,371
But a real company runs hundreds of containers on many machines.

29
00:01:58,933 --> 00:02:04,132
When a server dies at three in the morning, who restarts its containers somewhere else?

30
00:02:04,132 --> 00:02:07,120
When a new copy is needed, which machine has room?

31
00:02:07,667 --> 00:02:11,157
Who updates forty containers to a new version without downtime?

32
00:02:11,157 --> 00:02:14,703
And how do the containers find each other when they keep moving?

33
00:02:15,267 --> 00:02:18,109
Without an orchestrator, the answer is:

34
00:02:18,109 --> 00:02:19,931
you, by hand, every time.

35
00:02:19,931 --> 00:02:23,721
Kubernetes is the orchestrator that does it for you.

36
00:02:25,467 --> 00:02:27,026
The core idea is simple.

37
00:02:27,026 --> 00:02:32,028
You do not give Kubernetes commands like start this container on that server.

38
00:02:32,028 --> 00:02:34,171
You describe the result you want:

39
00:02:34,171 --> 00:02:37,030
the web application, two copies, this image.

40
00:02:37,567 --> 00:02:41,268
Kubernetes constantly observes what is really running.

41
00:02:41,268 --> 00:02:44,216
Say a node died, and only one copy is left.

42
00:02:44,767 --> 00:02:45,731
It compares:

43
00:02:45,731 --> 00:02:47,658
desired two, actual one.

44
00:02:47,658 --> 00:02:48,622
And it acts:

45
00:02:48,622 --> 00:02:52,156
it starts another copy on a healthy machine.

46
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:59,481
This loop, observe, compare, act, runs all the time, for every object in the cluster.

47
00:02:59,481 --> 00:03:01,714
It is called reconciliation.

48
00:03:02,267 --> 00:03:09,141
Self-healing, scheduling, service discovery, rolling updates and scaling all come from this one idea.

49
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:13,489
Here is what you are about to install.

50
00:03:13,489 --> 00:03:15,601
On the left, the control plane:

51
00:03:15,601 --> 00:03:17,305
the brain of the cluster.

52
00:03:17,305 --> 00:03:20,030
The A P I server is the only front door.

53
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:23,505
You, the nodes and every component talk only to it.

54
00:03:23,505 --> 00:03:27,389
Behind it, etcd stores the complete state of the cluster:

55
00:03:27,389 --> 00:03:29,364
everything you ever declared.

56
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:33,156
The scheduler decides on which node each new Pod runs.

57
00:03:33,156 --> 00:03:37,074
The controller manager runs the reconciliation loops we just saw.

58
00:03:37,633 --> 00:03:39,398
In a cloud there is one more:

59
00:03:39,398 --> 00:03:43,718
the cloud controller manager, which creates load balancers and volumes.

60
00:03:44,267 --> 00:03:45,892
On the right, every node.

61
00:03:45,892 --> 00:03:47,972
The kubelet is the node's agent:

62
00:03:47,972 --> 00:03:53,043
it watches the A P I server for Pods assigned to its node, and makes them run.

63
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:55,971
It does not run containers itself.

64
00:03:55,971 --> 00:04:01,339
It asks the container runtime, containerd, through an interface called C R I.

65
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:04,738
kube-proxy turns Services into network rules,

66
00:04:04,738 --> 00:04:10,288
and the C N I plugin gives every Pod its own I P address and connects Pods across nodes.

67
00:04:10,288 --> 00:04:11,928
Keep this picture in mind.

68
00:04:11,928 --> 00:04:15,081
We will install every one of these pieces by hand.

69
00:04:16,833 --> 00:04:19,721
Let's follow one command through the cluster.

70
00:04:19,721 --> 00:04:26,330
kube control apply sends your Deployment to the A P I server, which validates it and stores it in etcd.

71
00:04:26,330 --> 00:04:27,421
Nothing runs yet.

72
00:04:27,967 --> 00:04:33,226
The controller manager notices a Deployment without Pods, and creates the Pod objects.

73
00:04:33,767 --> 00:04:39,471
The scheduler notices Pods without a node, and assigns each one to a node with enough free resources.

74
00:04:40,033 --> 00:04:43,036
The kubelet on that node notices a Pod assigned to it,

75
00:04:43,036 --> 00:04:46,483
and asks containerd to pull the image and start the container.

76
00:04:47,033 --> 00:04:53,658
Finally the C N I gives the Pod an I P address, and kube-proxy makes the Service send traffic to it.

77
00:04:53,658 --> 00:04:58,030
Five components, each doing one job, all through the A P I server.

78
00:04:59,767 --> 00:05:03,012
Every installation method runs these same components.

79
00:05:03,012 --> 00:05:04,972
What changes is where they live.

80
00:05:04,972 --> 00:05:09,749
With kube admin, the control plane runs as Pods on your control-plane machine,

81
00:05:09,749 --> 00:05:12,076
and you are responsible for all of it.

82
00:05:12,633 --> 00:05:17,048
Minikube packs an entire node into one Docker container on your laptop.

83
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:22,232
MicroK8s runs all the components in a single process called kube light,

84
00:05:22,232 --> 00:05:24,971
and replaces etcd with a lighter database.

85
00:05:25,533 --> 00:05:30,083
And on EKS, AWS runs the control plane in its own account.

86
00:05:30,083 --> 00:05:31,809
You never even see it.

87
00:05:31,809 --> 00:05:34,163
You only run the worker nodes.

88
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:36,882
Time to build.

89
00:05:36,882 --> 00:05:39,406
For kube admin we need two machines:

90
00:05:39,406 --> 00:05:44,664
a control plane with two C P Us and four gigabytes of memory, and a worker.

91
00:05:44,664 --> 00:05:50,974
Multipass creates Ubuntu virtual machines with one command, on Windows, mac O S and Linux.

92
00:05:51,533 --> 00:05:55,476
Two machines, both running, each with its own I P address.

93
00:05:55,476 --> 00:05:58,468
Everything from here on happens inside them.

94
00:06:00,233 --> 00:06:05,468
Before Kubernetes, Linux needs four kinds of preparation, and each one has a reason.

95
00:06:05,468 --> 00:06:11,140
Swap goes off, because the kubelet's memory management assumes that memory limits are real.

96
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:12,871
Two kernel modules:

97
00:06:12,871 --> 00:06:16,261
overlay for container file systems, and B R net filter,

98
00:06:16,261 --> 00:06:22,609
so that traffic between Pods on a Linux bridge goes through I P tables, where Kubernetes Services live.

99
00:06:23,167 --> 00:06:24,464
Two kernel settings:

100
00:06:24,464 --> 00:06:27,707
I P forwarding, so the node can route Pod traffic,

101
00:06:27,707 --> 00:06:32,377
and the bridge setting that makes bridged traffic visible to I P tables.

102
00:06:32,377 --> 00:06:33,739
Remember I P forward.

103
00:06:33,739 --> 00:06:35,230
We will break it later.

104
00:06:35,767 --> 00:06:36,655
And the basics:

105
00:06:36,655 --> 00:06:39,083
synchronised clocks for the certificates,

106
00:06:39,083 --> 00:06:42,163
unique host names, and the ports the components use,

107
00:06:42,163 --> 00:06:46,901
like six four four three for the A P I server and ten two fifty for the kubelet.

108
00:06:48,667 --> 00:06:50,055
On both machines:

109
00:06:50,055 --> 00:06:53,078
swap off, now and after every reboot.

110
00:06:53,633 --> 00:06:58,463
Then the modules and the kernel settings, written to files so they survive a reboot.

111
00:06:58,463 --> 00:07:03,293
The check shows both modules loaded, and forwarding and bridge filtering set to one.

112
00:07:05,033 --> 00:07:06,600
Now the container runtime.

113
00:07:06,600 --> 00:07:10,035
We install containerd 2 from Docker's package repository.

114
00:07:10,035 --> 00:07:13,230
Then one detail that breaks many first installations:

115
00:07:13,230 --> 00:07:18,654
we write containerd's full default configuration and switch on the systemd C group driver,

116
00:07:18,654 --> 00:07:22,270
so containerd and the kubelet manage resources the same way.

117
00:07:22,833 --> 00:07:24,326
containerd is active.

118
00:07:24,326 --> 00:07:28,093
The kubelet will talk to it through the C R I socket.

119
00:07:28,633 --> 00:07:31,712
Finally kube admin, kubelet and kube control,

120
00:07:31,712 --> 00:07:35,954
version 1.37, from the official Kubernetes package repository,

121
00:07:35,954 --> 00:07:41,360
and held, so a routine system update can never upgrade the cluster by accident.

122
00:07:43,100 --> 00:07:46,857
kube admin init builds a control plane in phases.

123
00:07:46,857 --> 00:07:48,161
First, preflight:

124
00:07:48,161 --> 00:07:49,848
it checks the machine.

125
00:07:49,848 --> 00:07:55,292
C P Us, memory, swap, ports, and whether the container runtime answers.

126
00:07:55,292 --> 00:07:59,739
If anything is wrong, it stops before it changes anything.

127
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:05,059
Then it creates a private certificate authority and certificates for every component,

128
00:08:05,059 --> 00:08:06,851
plus the admin kube config file.

129
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:10,689
Then it writes the control plane, A P I server,

130
00:08:10,689 --> 00:08:15,167
scheduler, controller manager and etcd, as static Pod manifests.

131
00:08:15,167 --> 00:08:18,316
The kubelet sees those files and starts them.

132
00:08:18,867 --> 00:08:24,841
Finally it creates a token so workers can join, and installs Core D N S and kube-proxy.

133
00:08:26,600 --> 00:08:29,167
Here it is, on the real control plane.

134
00:08:29,167 --> 00:08:31,802
One command, with one important option:

135
00:08:31,802 --> 00:08:37,409
the Pod network range, ten two forty four, which our network plugin will use later.

136
00:08:37,967 --> 00:08:39,202
About a minute later:

137
00:08:39,202 --> 00:08:42,674
your Kubernetes control plane has initialized successfully.

138
00:08:42,674 --> 00:08:48,616
And at the end, the join command for workers, with a token and the hash of the cluster's certificate.

139
00:08:50,367 --> 00:08:55,473
We copy the admin kube config into our home folder, and ask for the nodes.

140
00:08:55,473 --> 00:08:57,336
The control plane is there.

141
00:08:57,336 --> 00:08:58,716
And it is Not Ready.

142
00:08:59,267 --> 00:09:02,184
Every beginner thinks something went wrong.

143
00:09:02,184 --> 00:09:05,982
Describe the node, and Kubernetes tells you exactly why:

144
00:09:05,982 --> 00:09:09,713
network plugin not ready, C N I plugin not initialized.

145
00:09:09,713 --> 00:09:10,934
Nothing is broken.

146
00:09:10,934 --> 00:09:13,580
Kubernetes does not ship a Pod network.

147
00:09:13,580 --> 00:09:16,158
You have to choose one and install it.

148
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:19,742
What does a C N I plugin do?

149
00:09:19,742 --> 00:09:24,545
Flannel, which we use, gives every node its own slice of the Pod network:

150
00:09:24,545 --> 00:09:30,006
ten two forty four zero on the control plane, ten two forty four one on the worker.

151
00:09:30,567 --> 00:09:35,741
On each node it creates a bridge for the local Pods, and a tunnel device called flannel one.

152
00:09:36,300 --> 00:09:39,041
When a Pod talks to a Pod on another node,

153
00:09:39,041 --> 00:09:43,806
Flannel wraps the packet in U D P and sends it across, a V X LAN overlay.

154
00:09:44,367 --> 00:09:46,950
The result is the Kubernetes network model:

155
00:09:46,950 --> 00:09:51,516
every Pod has its own I P, and every Pod can reach every other Pod directly.

156
00:09:51,516 --> 00:09:53,439
Remember the node in the middle:

157
00:09:53,439 --> 00:09:55,361
it has to forward those packets.

158
00:09:57,100 --> 00:09:59,633
One kube control apply installs Flannel:

159
00:09:59,633 --> 00:10:06,154
a namespace, permissions, its configuration, and a DaemonSet, which runs one Flannel Pod on every node.

160
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:09,911
Seconds later, the control plane is Ready.

161
00:10:10,467 --> 00:10:13,337
And now every system Pod is running:

162
00:10:13,337 --> 00:10:21,232
etcd, the A P I server, the controller manager, the scheduler, kube-proxy, Core D N S, and Flannel.

163
00:10:21,232 --> 00:10:24,421
That is the architecture diagram, alive.

164
00:10:26,167 --> 00:10:27,049
Now the worker.

165
00:10:27,049 --> 00:10:31,638
On the control plane we create a fresh join command, and run it on the worker.

166
00:10:31,638 --> 00:10:36,638
Behind the scenes the worker checks the control plane's certificate against the hash,

167
00:10:36,638 --> 00:10:40,815
authenticates with the token, and receives its own kubelet certificate.

168
00:10:40,815 --> 00:10:42,756
This node has joined the cluster.

169
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:44,950
A few seconds later:

170
00:10:44,950 --> 00:10:46,766
two nodes, both Ready.

171
00:10:46,766 --> 00:10:50,149
A real Kubernetes cluster, built by hand.

172
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:55,685
A cluster that says Ready is not yet a cluster you can trust.

173
00:10:55,685 --> 00:11:00,091
We use the same verification checklist on every cluster in this course.

174
00:11:00,091 --> 00:11:05,054
First, the nodes and the system Pods, and the health of the A P I server itself.

175
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:06,930
Then a real workload:

176
00:11:06,930 --> 00:11:11,614
two copies of nginx, and a Service that makes them reachable from outside.

177
00:11:12,167 --> 00:11:14,138
And the check most people forget:

178
00:11:14,138 --> 00:11:17,245
can a Pod on one node reach a Pod on the other node?

179
00:11:17,245 --> 00:11:19,456
That is the real test of the network.

180
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:23,747
The A P I server's own health checks:

181
00:11:23,747 --> 00:11:26,569
every check passed, ready z check passed.

182
00:11:27,133 --> 00:11:30,963
The web Deployment rolled out, two of two replicas available.

183
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:34,444
And through the NodePort on the worker's I P:

184
00:11:34,444 --> 00:11:35,752
welcome to engine x.

185
00:11:35,752 --> 00:11:40,396
The request went into the worker, through kube-proxy's rules, to a Pod.

186
00:11:42,133 --> 00:11:42,852
Last check.

187
00:11:42,852 --> 00:11:47,818
From the control plane, we fetch the page directly from a Pod's I P address.

188
00:11:47,818 --> 00:11:51,935
The packet leaves the control plane through the Flannel tunnel,

189
00:11:51,935 --> 00:11:55,202
arrives on the worker and is delivered to the Pod.

190
00:11:55,202 --> 00:11:56,639
The answer comes back.

191
00:11:56,639 --> 00:11:58,926
The Pod network works across nodes.

192
00:11:58,926 --> 00:12:00,495
The cluster is verified.

193
00:12:02,233 --> 00:12:03,413
Now we break it.

194
00:12:03,413 --> 00:12:05,183
Troubleshooting lab one:

195
00:12:05,183 --> 00:12:06,953
the worker is Not Ready.

196
00:12:06,953 --> 00:12:11,083
Someone stopped its kubelet, but we don't know that yet.

197
00:12:11,083 --> 00:12:12,853
All we see is Not Ready.

198
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:16,029
Describe the node and read the conditions.

199
00:12:16,029 --> 00:12:18,908
Every condition says Unknown, and the message:

200
00:12:18,908 --> 00:12:21,162
kubelet stopped posting node status.

201
00:12:21,162 --> 00:12:24,604
The control plane has not heard from this node's agent.

202
00:12:25,167 --> 00:12:28,016
So we look at the agent, on the worker:

203
00:12:28,016 --> 00:12:29,770
the kubelet is inactive.

204
00:12:29,770 --> 00:12:31,012
Root cause found.

205
00:12:31,012 --> 00:12:34,446
Start it, and the node is Ready within seconds.

206
00:12:36,200 --> 00:12:36,859
Lab three:

207
00:12:36,859 --> 00:12:42,661
a colleague prepared a new machine, and kube admin init fails immediately, in preflight.

208
00:12:42,661 --> 00:12:45,364
The error is about the container runtime.

209
00:12:45,900 --> 00:12:48,131
Is containerd running?

210
00:12:48,131 --> 00:12:49,347
Yes, active.

211
00:12:49,347 --> 00:12:51,679
So the process is fine.

212
00:12:52,233 --> 00:12:55,212
But the configuration file disables a plugin:

213
00:12:55,212 --> 00:12:55,609
C R I.

214
00:12:55,609 --> 00:12:58,124
The exact interface the kubelet needs.

215
00:12:58,124 --> 00:13:04,478
The containerd package from Docker's repository is configured for Docker, which doesn't need it.

216
00:13:05,033 --> 00:13:11,609
Write the full default configuration, restart, and crictl gets an answer from the runtime.

217
00:13:11,609 --> 00:13:12,851
Preflight passes.

218
00:13:12,851 --> 00:13:13,362
Lesson:

219
00:13:13,362 --> 00:13:15,920
running is not the same as working.

220
00:13:17,667 --> 00:13:18,330
Lab four:

221
00:13:18,330 --> 00:13:22,680
a join command from someone's old notes hangs in discovery.

222
00:13:22,680 --> 00:13:24,745
Debug it from the bottom up.

223
00:13:24,745 --> 00:13:28,505
Can the new machine reach port six four four three?

224
00:13:28,505 --> 00:13:29,243
Succeeded.

225
00:13:29,243 --> 00:13:31,381
Does the A P I server answer?

226
00:13:31,381 --> 00:13:33,003
Yes, with its version.

227
00:13:33,567 --> 00:13:34,982
Then the credentials:

228
00:13:34,982 --> 00:13:39,295
the token in the old command is not in the cluster's token list.

229
00:13:39,295 --> 00:13:41,857
Tokens expire after twenty-four hours.

230
00:13:41,857 --> 00:13:42,396
The fix:

231
00:13:42,396 --> 00:13:44,755
always create a fresh join command.

232
00:13:45,300 --> 00:13:49,119
For comparison, the other classic cause, a firewall.

233
00:13:49,119 --> 00:13:53,672
Block port six four four three, and the connection is refused.

234
00:13:53,672 --> 00:13:59,914
Two causes, the same hanging join, and one command, nc, tells them apart in a second.

235
00:14:01,667 --> 00:14:07,416
Minikube takes everything we just built by hand and puts it into a single Docker container on your computer.

236
00:14:07,967 --> 00:14:10,403
Inside that container is a complete node:

237
00:14:10,403 --> 00:14:13,256
the control plane and a kubelet with containerd.

238
00:14:13,800 --> 00:14:15,530
And here is the fun part:

239
00:14:15,530 --> 00:14:19,058
Minikube sets it up with kube admin, automatically.

240
00:14:19,058 --> 00:14:23,624
The same tool, the same components, in seconds instead of an hour.

241
00:14:24,167 --> 00:14:30,116
It writes a kube control context called minikube, so your normal kube control talks to it.

242
00:14:30,667 --> 00:14:34,240
On top, add-ons for common extras, and profiles:

243
00:14:34,240 --> 00:14:37,366
several independent clusters side by side.

244
00:14:39,100 --> 00:14:41,750
Installation is one binary.

245
00:14:41,750 --> 00:14:43,909
Minikube version 1.39.

246
00:14:44,467 --> 00:14:50,109
Then minikube start, with the Docker driver, two C P Us and four gigabytes.

247
00:14:50,109 --> 00:14:53,871
It prepares Kubernetes 1.37, configures the C N I,

248
00:14:53,871 --> 00:14:58,987
starts the components, and switches kube control to the new cluster.

249
00:14:58,987 --> 00:14:59,363
Done.

250
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:03,143
Same checklist as before.

251
00:15:03,143 --> 00:15:04,451
minikube status:

252
00:15:04,451 --> 00:15:09,599
host, kubelet and A P I server running, kube config configured.

253
00:15:10,133 --> 00:15:14,688
One node, Ready, called minikube, running containerd.

254
00:15:15,233 --> 00:15:16,745
And proof of what we said:

255
00:15:16,745 --> 00:15:19,943
the whole node is one Docker container on this machine.

256
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:25,299
The same web Deployment with two replicas, rolled out.

257
00:15:25,833 --> 00:15:32,287
minikube service web dash dash url gives us an address that reaches the NodePort inside the container.

258
00:15:32,287 --> 00:15:33,553
Welcome to engine x.

259
00:15:34,100 --> 00:15:40,626
And after enabling the metrics server add-on, kube control top shows how much C P U and memory the node uses.

260
00:15:42,367 --> 00:15:44,439
Profiles are separate clusters.

261
00:15:44,439 --> 00:15:45,976
This one has two nodes:

262
00:15:45,976 --> 00:15:49,586
two containers acting as a control plane and a worker.

263
00:15:50,133 --> 00:15:54,683
In lab two, a third cluster called prod-like runs alongside the main one.

264
00:15:55,233 --> 00:15:59,770
Because it runs Kubernetes 1.36, the version production still uses.

265
00:15:59,770 --> 00:16:04,375
Test against it before you upgrade, then delete it with one command.

266
00:16:06,133 --> 00:16:08,726
Troubleshooting lab eight happens here.

267
00:16:08,726 --> 00:16:11,253
A cluster with one gigabyte of memory?

268
00:16:11,253 --> 00:16:13,514
Minikube refuses before it starts:

269
00:16:13,514 --> 00:16:15,708
Kubernetes alone needs about two.

270
00:16:16,267 --> 00:16:19,534
Then a small web app that stays Pending.

271
00:16:19,534 --> 00:16:21,331
No container, no logs.

272
00:16:21,867 --> 00:16:25,958
The reason is in the Pod's events, written by the scheduler:

273
00:16:25,958 --> 00:16:28,550
no node available, insufficient C P U.

274
00:16:28,550 --> 00:16:31,891
And the Deployment asks for three hundred C P Us.

275
00:16:31,891 --> 00:16:37,483
Someone typed three hundred, instead of three hundred M, three hundred millicores.

276
00:16:37,483 --> 00:16:40,620
The scheduler counts requests, not real usage.

277
00:16:41,167 --> 00:16:44,313
Correct the unit, and it rolls out immediately.

278
00:16:46,067 --> 00:16:50,132
MicroK8s, from Canonical, takes a different approach.

279
00:16:50,132 --> 00:16:53,046
All of Kubernetes is one snap package.

280
00:16:53,600 --> 00:16:57,770
Inside, the control plane and node components run in one process,

281
00:16:57,770 --> 00:17:04,441
kube light, and the cluster state lives in D Q lite, a distributed version of S Q lite, instead of etcd.

282
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:12,259
A C N I, Calico, and Core D N S are ready right after the installation, and add-ons are one command each.

283
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:15,803
You choose the Kubernetes version with a channel.

284
00:17:15,803 --> 00:17:20,031
We pin 1.36 stable, the newest stable track at the time of recording,

285
00:17:20,031 --> 00:17:23,584
so updates never jump to a new minor version on their own.

286
00:17:25,333 --> 00:17:27,918
On a fresh Ubuntu machine:

287
00:17:27,918 --> 00:17:29,608
one snap install.

288
00:17:29,608 --> 00:17:32,293
MicroK8s 1.36 is installed.

289
00:17:32,833 --> 00:17:38,058
We add our user to the microk8s group, so we don't need sudo for every command.

290
00:17:38,600 --> 00:17:41,975
And microk8s status dash dash wait ready:

291
00:17:41,975 --> 00:17:42,633
running.

292
00:17:42,633 --> 00:17:45,843
No kube admin init, no C N I to choose.

293
00:17:45,843 --> 00:17:47,325
That is the trade:

294
00:17:47,325 --> 00:17:49,794
fewer decisions, less control.

295
00:17:51,533 --> 00:17:54,568
The node is Ready, running containerd 2.

296
00:17:55,133 --> 00:17:57,385
But look at the system Pods:

297
00:17:57,385 --> 00:17:59,557
only Calico and Core D N S.

298
00:17:59,557 --> 00:18:02,453
Where are the A P I server and etcd?

299
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,280
They are not Pods.

300
00:18:04,280 --> 00:18:07,766
They are system dee services managed by the snap:

301
00:18:07,766 --> 00:18:13,598
kube light, which runs the Kubernetes components, and k8s D Q lite, the datastore.

302
00:18:13,598 --> 00:18:16,443
Same architecture, packaged differently.

303
00:18:18,200 --> 00:18:21,059
The web workload answers through its NodePort.

304
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:23,390
Lab three goes one step further.

305
00:18:23,390 --> 00:18:26,692
The ingress add-on installs the Traefik ingress controller,

306
00:18:26,692 --> 00:18:30,776
and an Ingress rule sends the host name web dot local to the web Service.

307
00:18:31,333 --> 00:18:34,561
A request for web dot local, on port eighty:

308
00:18:34,561 --> 00:18:36,028
welcome to engine x.

309
00:18:36,567 --> 00:18:38,031
Any other host name:

310
00:18:38,031 --> 00:18:40,448
four oh four from the controller.

311
00:18:40,448 --> 00:18:42,865
One entry point, routing by name.

312
00:18:42,865 --> 00:18:44,916
That is what Ingress is for.

313
00:18:46,667 --> 00:18:47,743
Now the cloud.

314
00:18:47,743 --> 00:18:53,127
On Amazon EKS, AWS runs the control plane for you, in its own account.

315
00:18:53,127 --> 00:18:57,818
The A P I servers and etcd run in several availability zones,

316
00:18:57,818 --> 00:19:01,586
and AWS keeps them patched, scaled and backed up.

317
00:19:02,133 --> 00:19:03,187
That has a price:

318
00:19:03,187 --> 00:19:08,148
about ten cents per hour for the control plane alone, whether you use it or not.

319
00:19:08,700 --> 00:19:11,381
Everything else is yours, in your account:

320
00:19:11,381 --> 00:19:15,784
the network, the V P C with subnets in three zones and a nat gateway,

321
00:19:16,333 --> 00:19:20,878
the worker nodes, here a managed node group of two E C2 instances,

322
00:19:21,433 --> 00:19:22,035
and I A M:

323
00:19:22,035 --> 00:19:27,508
which roles the cluster and the nodes use, and which people may do what inside the cluster.

324
00:19:29,267 --> 00:19:32,321
Before we create anything in the cloud, four rules.

325
00:19:32,321 --> 00:19:34,178
It costs money while it exists:

326
00:19:34,178 --> 00:19:37,531
about twenty-five to thirty cents per hour for this lab.

327
00:19:37,531 --> 00:19:41,843
Cheap for an afternoon, around two hundred dollars a month if forgotten.

328
00:19:42,400 --> 00:19:47,824
Everything is created in one region, from one file, and tagged with the project name.

329
00:19:48,367 --> 00:19:53,336
We count what exists in the region before we start, and again after cleanup.

330
00:19:53,336 --> 00:19:54,840
The numbers must match.

331
00:19:55,400 --> 00:19:59,804
And the teardown is verified by a script that checks every resource type.

332
00:19:59,804 --> 00:20:02,579
It is part of the lesson, not an afterthought.

333
00:20:04,333 --> 00:20:04,699
First:

334
00:20:04,699 --> 00:20:07,438
which identity and which region are we using?

335
00:20:07,438 --> 00:20:11,700
The account number and the user name are masked in everything you see.

336
00:20:12,233 --> 00:20:16,321
Then the inventory of the region, before we start.

337
00:20:16,321 --> 00:20:22,617
No EKS clusters, one V P C, no nat gateways, no load balancers, no instances.

338
00:20:22,617 --> 00:20:24,825
Keep these numbers in mind.

339
00:20:26,567 --> 00:20:33,769
This is the whole cluster, in one file for eksctl, the official command-line tool for EKS.

340
00:20:33,769 --> 00:20:38,491
The name, the region, version 1.37, and tags on everything.

341
00:20:39,033 --> 00:20:44,815
A new V P C with its own address range, and a single nat gateway, enough for a lab.

342
00:20:44,815 --> 00:20:50,318
The A P I endpoint is public, for our kube control, and private, for the nodes.

343
00:20:50,867 --> 00:20:53,822
Access entries decide who may use the cluster.

344
00:20:53,822 --> 00:20:56,906
The creator automatically becomes cluster admin.

345
00:20:57,467 --> 00:20:58,784
And the node group:

346
00:20:58,784 --> 00:21:05,091
two T3 medium instances with twenty gigabyte disks, in private subnets, with no public I P.

347
00:21:06,833 --> 00:21:13,183
Before we run eksctl, let's see the console path, recorded live in a read-only session.

348
00:21:13,183 --> 00:21:17,708
In Elastic Kubernetes Service, clusters, click create cluster.

349
00:21:18,267 --> 00:21:20,239
Choose custom configuration.

350
00:21:20,239 --> 00:21:26,721
Quick configuration would create an EKS Auto Mode cluster, where AWS also manages the nodes.

351
00:21:27,267 --> 00:21:29,052
Auto Mode is on by default.

352
00:21:29,052 --> 00:21:29,978
Switch it off:

353
00:21:29,978 --> 00:21:34,938
we want the classic setup with a managed node group that we size ourselves.

354
00:21:36,700 --> 00:21:43,653
Enter the name, and choose the cluster I A M role, the role EKS uses to manage AWS resources for you.

355
00:21:43,653 --> 00:21:47,094
Create new role opens I A M with the right policy.

356
00:21:47,633 --> 00:21:50,469
The console pre-selects version 1.36.

357
00:21:50,469 --> 00:21:52,615
Open the list and pick 1.37.

358
00:21:52,615 --> 00:21:57,366
Each version shows when its standard and extended support end.

359
00:21:57,900 --> 00:21:58,898
Cluster access:

360
00:21:58,898 --> 00:22:05,286
allow cluster administrator access for your own identity, and the EKS A P I authentication mode.

361
00:22:07,033 --> 00:22:08,307
Next, networking:

362
00:22:08,307 --> 00:22:12,501
a V P C, and subnets in at least two availability zones.

363
00:22:12,501 --> 00:22:15,647
The console pre-selects the default V P C.

364
00:22:15,647 --> 00:22:18,493
eksctl builds a dedicated one instead.

365
00:22:19,033 --> 00:22:20,105
Then add-ons.

366
00:22:20,105 --> 00:22:25,548
The essential ones are the V P C C N I, Core D N S and kube-proxy.

367
00:22:26,100 --> 00:22:29,189
And at the end of the review, the create button.

368
00:22:29,189 --> 00:22:30,412
We do not click it.

369
00:22:30,412 --> 00:22:36,396
Ours is created from the file, so it can be reviewed, repeated, and deleted with one command.

370
00:22:38,133 --> 00:22:39,039
Now for real.

371
00:22:39,039 --> 00:22:42,382
eksctl create cluster dash f cluster dot yammel.

372
00:22:42,382 --> 00:22:45,794
It turns the file into two CloudFormation stacks:

373
00:22:45,794 --> 00:22:49,973
one for the cluster and its network, one for the node group.

374
00:22:50,533 --> 00:22:53,470
Fourteen and a half minutes later, measured:

375
00:22:53,470 --> 00:22:57,208
two nodes ready, and the cluster k8s from zero is ready.

376
00:22:58,967 --> 00:23:01,410
The same checklist, on EKS.

377
00:23:01,410 --> 00:23:07,836
The control plane is active, version 1.37, platform version e k s four.

378
00:23:08,400 --> 00:23:13,565
Two worker nodes, Ready, Amazon Linux 2023 with containerd.

379
00:23:13,565 --> 00:23:15,753
Notice the I P addresses:

380
00:23:15,753 --> 00:23:18,030
ten fifty, from our V P C.

381
00:23:18,567 --> 00:23:20,383
And the system Pods.

382
00:23:20,383 --> 00:23:22,654
No A P I server, no etcd:

383
00:23:22,654 --> 00:23:25,107
those run in AWS's account.

384
00:23:25,107 --> 00:23:26,742
Instead, aws-node:

385
00:23:26,742 --> 00:23:32,374
the Amazon V P C C N I, which gives Pods real V P C addresses.

386
00:23:32,374 --> 00:23:35,281
No overlay network like Flannel.

387
00:23:37,033 --> 00:23:38,514
Our web Deployment:

388
00:23:38,514 --> 00:23:41,943
two Pods on two nodes, with V P C addresses.

389
00:23:42,500 --> 00:23:45,720
This time the Service is of type LoadBalancer.

390
00:23:45,720 --> 00:23:50,899
Kubernetes asks AWS for a real load balancer, and gives us its D N S name.

391
00:23:51,433 --> 00:23:57,054
From the internet, through the AWS load balancer, to a node port, to a Pod:

392
00:23:57,054 --> 00:23:58,553
welcome to engine x.

393
00:24:00,300 --> 00:24:02,888
The same cluster in the console:

394
00:24:02,888 --> 00:24:08,955
active, version 1.37, its A P I endpoint and the I A M role eksctl created.

395
00:24:09,500 --> 00:24:11,949
The compute tab shows the node group.

396
00:24:11,949 --> 00:24:14,331
But the node list says unauthorized!

397
00:24:14,331 --> 00:24:18,369
Our console session is read-only and has no EKS access entry.

398
00:24:18,369 --> 00:24:23,663
The console reads nodes through the Kubernetes A P I, and Kubernetes refuses it.

399
00:24:23,663 --> 00:24:27,634
An AWS permission alone does not let you inside the cluster.

400
00:24:28,200 --> 00:24:31,174
The access tab shows who does have entries:

401
00:24:31,174 --> 00:24:35,255
the node role, an AWS service role, and our admin identity.

402
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:42,334
Outside EKS, CloudFormation shows the two stacks eksctl created.

403
00:24:42,900 --> 00:24:47,820
E C2 shows the two worker instances, in two different availability zones.

404
00:24:48,367 --> 00:24:52,219
And the load balancer that Kubernetes created for our Service.

405
00:24:52,219 --> 00:24:52,965
Remember it:

406
00:24:52,965 --> 00:24:57,811
it is not part of eksctl's stacks, and that matters when we delete everything.

407
00:24:59,567 --> 00:25:03,220
Five more labs, and one method for all of them.

408
00:25:03,220 --> 00:25:05,940
First, what exactly is the symptom?

409
00:25:05,940 --> 00:25:07,728
Read the status column.

410
00:25:08,267 --> 00:25:11,250
Second, what does Kubernetes know about it?

411
00:25:11,250 --> 00:25:15,136
Describe the object, and read its conditions and events.

412
00:25:15,700 --> 00:25:18,541
Third, what does the component itself say?

413
00:25:18,541 --> 00:25:23,140
Container logs, or the system journal of the kubelet and containerd.

414
00:25:23,700 --> 00:25:25,927
Fourth, which layer is broken:

415
00:25:25,927 --> 00:25:32,535
the network, the runtime, the kubelet, the A P I server, or the workload's configuration?

416
00:25:33,100 --> 00:25:38,406
And fifth, change one thing, and verify with the same command that showed the problem.

417
00:25:40,167 --> 00:25:42,151
Lab two is the nasty one.

418
00:25:42,151 --> 00:25:45,484
Every node is Ready, every Pod is Running.

419
00:25:45,484 --> 00:25:47,071
Nothing looks wrong.

420
00:25:47,633 --> 00:25:52,015
But from the control plane, a Pod on the worker does not answer.

421
00:25:52,015 --> 00:25:53,521
The request times out.

422
00:25:54,067 --> 00:25:58,416
The Pod answers inside itself, and Flannel's devices exist.

423
00:25:58,416 --> 00:26:00,407
So, the node in the middle:

424
00:26:00,407 --> 00:26:02,693
is it still forwarding packets?

425
00:26:02,693 --> 00:26:04,167
I P forward is zero.

426
00:26:04,167 --> 00:26:05,936
Someone switched it off.

427
00:26:05,936 --> 00:26:11,687
sysctl dash dash system restores the saved value, and the request works again.

428
00:26:11,687 --> 00:26:14,710
This is why we test traffic across nodes.

429
00:26:16,467 --> 00:26:17,087
Lab five.

430
00:26:17,087 --> 00:26:19,842
The two most common kube control errors.

431
00:26:19,842 --> 00:26:25,836
localhost eight thousand eighty refused means kube control found no kube config at all.

432
00:26:26,400 --> 00:26:30,415
A timeout to an address means the kube config points at the wrong server.

433
00:26:30,967 --> 00:26:34,018
The first command when kube control misbehaves:

434
00:26:34,018 --> 00:26:35,901
config view dash dash minify.

435
00:26:35,901 --> 00:26:39,536
It shows exactly where kube control is going to connect.

436
00:26:41,300 --> 00:26:41,956
Lab six.

437
00:26:41,956 --> 00:26:45,808
Someone tidied up the Core D N S configuration.

438
00:26:45,808 --> 00:26:49,660
The new Core D N S Pods crash, again and again:

439
00:26:49,660 --> 00:26:51,300
crash loop back off.

440
00:26:51,833 --> 00:26:57,300
Crash loop back off is not the cause, it only means the container keeps exiting.

441
00:26:57,300 --> 00:27:00,648
The cause is in the logs of the last crashed run:

442
00:27:00,648 --> 00:27:03,655
an unknown directive, forwardd, with two Ds.

443
00:27:03,655 --> 00:27:04,270
One typo.

444
00:27:04,833 --> 00:27:11,233
Restore the backup, restart Core D N S, and a test Pod resolves the web Service's name again.

445
00:27:12,967 --> 00:27:13,671
Lab seven.

446
00:27:13,671 --> 00:27:15,783
The worker is Not Ready again.

447
00:27:15,783 --> 00:27:19,022
Same symptom as lab one, but read the message:

448
00:27:19,022 --> 00:27:23,881
this time the kubelet is reporting, and it reports a runtime problem.

449
00:27:24,433 --> 00:27:25,350
On the node:

450
00:27:25,350 --> 00:27:28,865
the kubelet is active, containerd is inactive.

451
00:27:29,400 --> 00:27:34,195
And the kubelet's journal shows it failing to reach containerd's socket.

452
00:27:34,195 --> 00:27:36,858
Start containerd, and the node is Ready.

453
00:27:36,858 --> 00:27:38,856
Same symptom, different layer:

454
00:27:38,856 --> 00:27:40,455
always read the message.

455
00:27:42,200 --> 00:27:44,441
Let's put the four side by side.

456
00:27:44,441 --> 00:27:49,623
kube admin is for learning and for running Kubernetes on your own servers.

457
00:27:49,623 --> 00:27:51,654
Minikube for laptops and C I.

458
00:27:51,654 --> 00:27:54,735
MicroK8s for edge devices and small servers.

459
00:27:54,735 --> 00:27:56,556
EKS for production on AWS.

460
00:27:57,100 --> 00:27:58,591
The control plane:

461
00:27:58,591 --> 00:28:05,386
static Pods you own, a container, a single process, or invisible in AWS's account.

462
00:28:05,386 --> 00:28:06,546
The datastore:

463
00:28:06,546 --> 00:28:09,197
etcd everywhere except MicroK8s.

464
00:28:09,197 --> 00:28:11,103
And the network plugin:

465
00:28:11,103 --> 00:28:15,246
your choice, built in, Calico, or the V P C C N I.

466
00:28:15,800 --> 00:28:16,979
High availability:

467
00:28:16,979 --> 00:28:20,712
three control planes and a load balancer with kube admin,

468
00:28:20,712 --> 00:28:25,690
none for Minikube, automatic with three MicroK8s nodes, and built in on EKS.

469
00:28:25,690 --> 00:28:28,900
Upgrades are yours, except the EKS control plane.

470
00:28:29,433 --> 00:28:31,353
Cost and production use.

471
00:28:31,353 --> 00:28:32,393
Free locally.

472
00:28:32,393 --> 00:28:38,231
On EKS, about thirty cents per hour, and in return you operate much less.

473
00:28:39,967 --> 00:28:42,671
Lab five turns this into decisions.

474
00:28:42,671 --> 00:28:47,231
A developer testing a chart on a laptop, or a C I pipeline:

475
00:28:47,231 --> 00:28:47,926
Minikube.

476
00:28:48,467 --> 00:28:52,650
A factory with forty small machines, often offline:

477
00:28:52,650 --> 00:28:53,388
MicroK8s.

478
00:28:53,388 --> 00:28:59,048
A start-up with a customer A P I on AWS and no Kubernetes specialist:

479
00:28:59,048 --> 00:28:59,376
EKS.

480
00:28:59,933 --> 00:29:05,972
A bank that must own everything in its own data centre, or you, preparing for the C K A exam:

481
00:29:05,972 --> 00:29:06,686
kube admin.

482
00:29:06,686 --> 00:29:08,699
Most companies use two at once:

483
00:29:08,699 --> 00:29:12,985
a local cluster for development, and a managed one for production.

484
00:29:14,733 --> 00:29:15,553
The capstone.

485
00:29:15,553 --> 00:29:20,723
On Monday morning, the team's kube admin cluster stopped working over the weekend.

486
00:29:20,723 --> 00:29:22,678
There are three faults at once:

487
00:29:22,678 --> 00:29:29,235
kube control points at the wrong server, containerd is stopped on the worker, and I P forwarding is off.

488
00:29:29,800 --> 00:29:33,002
Find them from the symptoms, as if you didn't know.

489
00:29:33,002 --> 00:29:33,315
Hint:

490
00:29:33,315 --> 00:29:35,952
fix the layer that hides the others first.

491
00:29:35,952 --> 00:29:40,723
You cannot investigate nodes while kube control cannot even reach the A P I.

492
00:29:41,267 --> 00:29:47,267
When the nodes look healthy, run the full checklist, including Pod to Pod traffic across nodes.

493
00:29:47,267 --> 00:29:49,541
That is where the third fault hides.

494
00:29:50,100 --> 00:29:52,232
And finish like a professional:

495
00:29:52,232 --> 00:29:53,745
a short incident note.

496
00:29:53,745 --> 00:29:59,385
What broke, the impact, how you found it, how you fixed it, and how to prevent it.

497
00:29:59,933 --> 00:30:02,759
The capstone also has fifteen questions.

498
00:30:02,759 --> 00:30:04,595
Answer them without notes.

499
00:30:04,595 --> 00:30:07,703
If you can, you have understood this course.

500
00:30:07,703 --> 00:30:09,611
The scenario is tested too:

501
00:30:09,611 --> 00:30:12,083
it runs in C I on the real cluster.

502
00:30:13,833 --> 00:30:16,150
Time to clean up, and to prove it.

503
00:30:16,150 --> 00:30:17,308
kube admin first:

504
00:30:17,308 --> 00:30:23,168
drain the worker, so its Pods move away cleanly, and delete the node from the cluster.

505
00:30:23,733 --> 00:30:29,662
kube admin reset on both machines removes the control plane, etcd's data and the certificates.

506
00:30:29,662 --> 00:30:33,951
It does not remove the C N I configuration and the I P tables rules:

507
00:30:33,951 --> 00:30:35,528
the lesson does that too.

508
00:30:36,067 --> 00:30:36,503
Proof:

509
00:30:36,503 --> 00:30:39,773
the A P I server is gone, connection refused.

510
00:30:39,773 --> 00:30:43,551
Then multipass delete and purge remove the machines.

511
00:30:45,300 --> 00:30:45,922
Minikube:

512
00:30:45,922 --> 00:30:50,965
one command removes the container, its disk and its kube control context.

513
00:30:51,500 --> 00:30:52,502
And we check:

514
00:30:52,502 --> 00:30:54,815
no Kubernetes containers left.

515
00:30:55,367 --> 00:30:55,967
MicroK8s:

516
00:30:55,967 --> 00:31:01,836
snap remove with purge removes the package and all its data, without keeping a snapshot.

517
00:31:02,400 --> 00:31:04,690
And the machine itself is deleted.

518
00:31:06,433 --> 00:31:08,736
EKS, and here the order matters.

519
00:31:08,736 --> 00:31:11,469
First delete the LoadBalancer Service.

520
00:31:11,469 --> 00:31:15,714
The load balancer was created by Kubernetes, not by eksctl.

521
00:31:15,714 --> 00:31:19,815
If the cluster disappears first, the load balancer stays,

522
00:31:19,815 --> 00:31:23,988
keeps costing money, and blocks the deletion of the V P C.

523
00:31:24,533 --> 00:31:26,651
Then eksctl delete cluster.

524
00:31:26,651 --> 00:31:29,083
Eleven minutes later, measured:

525
00:31:29,083 --> 00:31:31,828
all cluster resources were deleted.

526
00:31:32,367 --> 00:31:34,759
Never trust it should be gone.

527
00:31:34,759 --> 00:31:38,825
The verification script checks every resource type:

528
00:31:38,825 --> 00:31:42,811
cluster, stacks, V P C, nat gateway, elastic I Ps,

529
00:31:42,811 --> 00:31:48,233
instances, volumes, load balancers, security groups and I A M roles.

530
00:31:48,233 --> 00:31:48,711
Clean.

531
00:31:50,467 --> 00:31:51,586
And the final check:

532
00:31:51,586 --> 00:31:56,290
the inventory of the region after cleanup is identical to the one before we started.

533
00:31:56,833 --> 00:31:59,651
The cluster existed for about forty-one minutes:

534
00:31:59,651 --> 00:32:05,050
fourteen and a half to create, our tests and the console tour, and eleven minutes to delete.

535
00:32:05,600 --> 00:32:11,265
At on-demand list prices, the whole EKS lesson cost roughly fifteen to twenty cents.

536
00:32:11,800 --> 00:32:13,448
Nothing was left behind.

537
00:32:13,448 --> 00:32:16,675
That is what responsible cloud work looks like.

538
00:32:18,433 --> 00:32:19,497
Let's look back.

539
00:32:19,497 --> 00:32:20,626
You started with:

540
00:32:20,626 --> 00:32:22,886
I have never installed Kubernetes.

541
00:32:22,886 --> 00:32:28,668
Now you can explain the architecture, and what happens when you run kube control apply.

542
00:32:29,233 --> 00:32:32,240
You have installed Kubernetes four ways:

543
00:32:32,240 --> 00:32:37,728
by hand with kube admin, with Minikube, with MicroK8s, and on Amazon EKS.

544
00:32:38,267 --> 00:32:43,667
You can verify any cluster with one checklist, including the test most people forget:

545
00:32:43,667 --> 00:32:45,001
traffic across nodes.

546
00:32:45,567 --> 00:32:50,466
You have found and fixed eight real installation problems, always with the same method.

547
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:54,970
And you know which method to choose, and how to leave nothing behind.

548
00:32:56,733 --> 00:32:57,828
Your next steps.

549
00:32:57,828 --> 00:33:02,002
Follow the tutorial folder, and build every cluster yourself.

550
00:33:02,002 --> 00:33:06,040
The outputs in the lessons show you exactly what to expect.

551
00:33:06,600 --> 00:33:11,118
Do the labs and the capstone before you open the solutions.

552
00:33:11,118 --> 00:33:12,037
Then repeat:

553
00:33:12,037 --> 00:33:16,478
understand, install, verify, break, troubleshoot, compare.

554
00:33:16,478 --> 00:33:19,541
Thanks for watching, and happy building.
